      "title": "Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "6b696019-9ef9-5a9f-8c76-7afb4ca4c152",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=({x}^{3}+4)\\ln(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n$f^{\\prime}(x)=3 x^{2} \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=({x}^{3}+4)$ and $v(x)=\\ln(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(({x}^{3}+4))^{′}\\ln(x)+({x}^{3}+4)(\\ln(x))^{′} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2})(\\ln(x))+({x}^{3}+4)(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\ln(x)+\\frac{{x}^{3}+4}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "f0ceb3cd-78d5-5463-9bd4-cc533c3299a3",
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "d878f2b3-3ea4-512c-a5ba-c3835cffcadf",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "\\frac{x^{3} + 4}{x}"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "d878f2b3-3ea4-512c-a5ba-c3835cffcadf"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
